+{\color{\revcolor}Fifth, the coverage demonstrated here refers to the
+main-campaign conditions, with the kinematic change cue available to the
+gate. In an exploratory post-hoc diagnostic with frozen weights and
+thresholds (twelve seeds, both gated configurations), zeroing the kinematic
+cue delivered to the gate from the second gating decision onward --- with the
+LSTM inputs left intact --- produced persistent suppression and
+$\mathrm{FR}=1.0$ in all evaluated configurations: once updates stop, the
+emitted probability, and hence the entropy consumed by the gate, remain
+frozen, and the current implementation has no reactivation mechanism
+independent of that cue. The diagnostic protocol, code, per-seed results,
+per-episode and per-decision logs, and a SHA-256 manifest are archived with
+the paper's research artifact, together with the frozen checkpoints and test
+arrays required to re-run it. This result does not characterize sensor failures in
+general; it identifies an operational limitation of the current gate
+implementation, whose mitigation (e.g., a mandatory periodic update) would
+modify the method and is left as future work.}
